      ⍝ Regression: Bugs25 #4 (Blake McBride). File_or_String::
      ⍝ scanf_long_long() accepted the lookahead character
      ⍝ unconditionally (any character, not just a digit) before
      ⍝ filtering the rest of the input, so a failed %d/%x conversion
      ⍝ invented a bogus 0 instead of reporting "no conversion" --
      ⍝ strtoll() only sets errno on overflow, never on "no digits
      ⍝ converted". Fixed in Quad_FIO.cc by filtering the lookahead the
      ⍝ same way the rest of the digit loop already does, and by
      ⍝ checking strtoll()'s endptr for "nothing consumed". Also made
      ⍝ %x/%X hex-letter-aware (0-9a-fA-F, not just 0-9) in both the
      ⍝ lookahead and the loop, fixing multi-digit hex conversions
      ⍝ (e.g. 'ff') as a side effect of the same code path.
      ⍝
      ⍴'%d' ⎕FIO[55] 'xyz'
0

      '%d %d %d' ⎕FIO[55] '12 xyz'
12

      '%x' ⎕FIO[55] 'zz'


      '%d' ⎕FIO[55] '-'


      '%d' ⎕FIO[55] '42'
42

      '%d' ⎕FIO[55] '¯42'
¯42

      '%x' ⎕FIO[55] 'ff'
255

      '%x' ⎕FIO[55] 'FF'
255

      ⍝ Bugs28 #49 regression: a suppressed conversion (%*d) ended the
      ⍝ scan outright instead of continuing with the next directive --
      ⍝ do_scanf()'s integer/float branches took the "scan failed" path
      ⍝ (goto out) whenever suppress was set, even on a successful
      ⍝ count==1, discarding every directive after the %*d. Fixed by
      ⍝ separating "did the scan itself succeed" (count==1, controls
      ⍝ whether to continue) from "should the result be kept"
      ⍝ (!suppress, controls whether to append to Z).
      ⍝
      '%*d %d' ⎕FIO[55] '1 2'
2

      ⍝ control: a suppressed %s was already correct
      ⍝
      '%*s %d' ⎕FIO[55] 'ab 2'
2

      '%d %*d %d' ⎕FIO[55] '1 2 3'
1 3

      '%*d %*d %d' ⎕FIO[55] '1 2 3'
3

      ⍝ regression (Bugs28 #100(j)): a field width in an integer/float
      ⍝ scanf conversion was silently ignored -- File_or_String::
      ⍝ scanf_long_long()/scanf_double() (Quad_FIO.cc) never received
      ⍝ do_scanf()'s own conv_len, unlike %c/%s, which already honoured
      ⍝ it. '%5d' on '1234567' used to consume and return the whole
      ⍝ 1234567 instead of stopping at the first 5 characters.
      ⍝
      '%5d' ⎕FIO[55] '1234567'
12345

      '%d' ⎕FIO[55] '1234567'
1234567

      '%3f' ⎕FIO[55] '12.34567'
12

      ⍝ control: %s already honoured field width before this fix
      ⍝
      '%2s' ⎕FIO[55] '1234567'
 12 

      ⍝ execve
      ⍝
      ⍝Filename←'/usr/local/lib/apl/TLV_server'  ⍝ whereever TLV_server lives
      Filename←'../tools/TLV_server'
      Handle ← FIO∆execve Filename        ⍝ start & connect TLV_server
      TLV ← 33 ⎕CR 42,'Forty-Two'         ⍝ encode a TLV buffer, Tag 42
      ⊣TLV ⎕FIO[43] Handle                ⍝ send TLV buffer to TLV_server
      TL ← 8 ⎕FIO[6] Handle               ⍝ read tag/lenght from TLV_server
      Value ← (256⊥4↓TL) ⎕FIO[6] Handle   ⍝ read value  from TLV_server
      34 ⎕CR TL,Value                     ⍝ display response tag and value
¯42 owT-ytroF
      TLV ← 33 ⎕CR 99,'quit'              ⍝ encode stop command, Tag 99
      ⊣TLV ⎕FIO[43] Handle                ⍝ send TLV buffer to TLV_server
      ⊣(⎕FIO[4] Handle)                   ⍝ close connection (stops TLV_server)

      ⍝ Regression: Bugs25 #9 (Blake McBride). fio_def(30, getcwd) in
      ⍝ Quad_FIO.def advertises ⎕FIO[30]/⎕FIO['getcwd']/⎕FIO.getcwd as
      ⍝ equivalent to ⎕FIO 30 (via ⎕FIO ⍬'s listing and the name lookup
      ⍝ table), but only eval_B() (the ⎕FIO 30 form) had an
      ⍝ implementation -- eval_XB() had no case 30, so the other three
      ⍝ advertised spellings all fell into default: and reported
      ⍝ themselves as invalid. Fixed in Quad_FIO.cc by adding case 30 to
      ⍝ eval_XB() too. The actual path is host-dependent, so compare
      ⍝ against the known-working ⎕FIO 30 form instead of a literal path.
      ⍝
      CWD←⎕FIO 30
      CWD ≡ ⎕FIO[30] ⍬
1
      CWD1←⎕FIO['getcwd'] 0
      CWD ≡ CWD1
1
      CWD2←⎕FIO.getcwd ⍬
      CWD ≡ CWD2
1

      ⍝ Regression: found while writing the tests just above (NOT one of
      ⍝ Blake's Bugs25 findings). Prefix::reduce_MISC_F_C_B() -- the F-C-B
      ⍝ shift-reduce phrase for "something f[C]B" where f[C]B itself has
      ⍝ a function/operand to its left -- special-cases ⎕FIO[49] (the one
      ⍝ ⎕FIO subfunction that is an operator) by calling
      ⍝ C->get_sole_integer() to compare the axis against 49. But ⎕FIO
      ⍝ axes can also be spelled as subfunction name strings (e.g.
      ⍝ 'strerror', 'getcwd' -- see Quad_FIO::value_to_subfun()), and
      ⍝ get_sole_integer() throws LENGTH_ERROR for any value with more
      ⍝ than one element. So any ⎕FIO[STRING] call with so much as a
      ⍝ monadic function to its left raised a spurious LENGTH ERROR,
      ⍝ even though the identical call with nothing to its left worked
      ⍝ fine (a different, unaffected reduce phrase handles that case).
      ⍝ Fixed in Prefix.cc by checking C->element_count()==1 before
      ⍝ calling get_sole_integer() on it.
      ⍝
      1 ≡ ⎕FIO['strerror'] 0
0

      ⍴⎕FIO['strerror'] 0
7

      1 ≡ ⎕FIO['getcwd'] 0
0

      FIO∆mkdir_777 FIO∆getcwd,'/test_data'
0
