      ⍝ Regression: Bugs25 #5 (Blake McBride). Quad_FIO::do_snprintf()'s
      ⍝ integer conversion branch strips any user-supplied length
      ⍝ modifier (h/l/L/q/j/z/t) and forces a 64-bit one, since APL
      ⍝ integers are always 64 bits -- but the float branch appended the
      ⍝ conversion char straight onto whatever modifier the user typed,
      ⍝ unfiltered. '%Lf' etc. then told snprintf() to read a long
      ⍝ double off the vararg slot holding a plain double (APL_Float) --
      ⍝ undefined behaviour, observed as spurious 'nan' and confirmed via
      ⍝ ASan's printf interceptor. Fixed in Quad_FIO.cc by stripping
      ⍝ length modifiers in the float branch too (no replacement is
      ⍝ added back: no modifier is correct for a plain double).
      ⍝
      '%f' ⎕FIO[58] 1.5
1.500000

      '%Lf' ⎕FIO[58] 1.5
1.500000

      '%Le' ⎕FIO[58] 1.5
1.500000e+00

      '%Lg' ⎕FIO[58] 1.5
1.5

      '%hf' ⎕FIO[58] 1.5
1.500000

      ⍝ regression (Bugs28 #100(j)): do_snprintf()'s integer and float
      ⍝ conversion branches called get_real_value() on a genuinely
      ⍝ complex argument without checking is_near_real() first --
      ⍝ ComplexCell::get_real_value() unconditionally returns just the
      ⍝ real part, silently discarding the imaginary part, instead of
      ⍝ the DOMAIN ERROR every other scalar-function complex-argument
      ⍝ check in this codebase already gives.
      ⍝
      '%d' ⎕FIO[58] 1J2
DOMAIN ERROR+
      '%d' ⎕FIO[58]1J2
      ^    ^

      )MORE
argument 0 is complex and cannot be used with integer format '%d' in A ⎕FIO.snprintf B
      )SIC

      '%f' ⎕FIO[58] 1J2
DOMAIN ERROR+
      '%f' ⎕FIO[58]1J2
      ^    ^

      )MORE
argument 0 is complex and cannot be used with format '%f' in A ⎕FIO.snprintf B
      )SIC

      ⍝ control: a tolerantly real complex argument is unaffected
      ⍝
      '%d' ⎕FIO[58] 1J0
1

      '%f' ⎕FIO[58] 1.5J0.0000000001
1.500000

      ⍝ Regression: Bugs25 #8 (Blake McBride). eval_AXB__202()/
      ⍝ eval_XB__202() (⎕FIO[202]/⎕FIO[203], get/set monadic/dyadic
      ⍝ threshold) called Tokenizer::tokenize_function() on an
      ⍝ arbitrary user character and then checked tok.is_function() --
      ⍝ but tokenize_function()'s own fall-through for a non-function
      ⍝ character is Assert(0 && "Missing Function"), so that check was
      ⍝ unreachable dead code and the assertion fired instead of a clean
      ⍝ DOMAIN ERROR. Fixed in Quad_FIO.cc by pre-filtering with
      ⍝ Avec::uni_to_token()'s is_function() (safe to call before the
      ⍝ token's function pointer is set, since is_function() only reads
      ⍝ the tag) instead of relying on tokenize_function()'s own check.
      ⍝
      ⎕FIO[202] 'x'
DOMAIN ERROR
      ⎕FIO[202]'x'
      ^

      )SIC

      ⎕FIO[202] ''
DOMAIN ERROR
      ⎕FIO[202]''
      ^

      )SIC

      ⎕FIO[202] '∇'
DOMAIN ERROR
      ⎕FIO[202]'∇'
      ^

      )SIC

      1 ⎕FIO[203] ''
DOMAIN ERROR
      1 ⎕FIO[203]''
      ^ ^

      )SIC

      ⍝ valid function unaffected, and reset + back to its default
      ⍝ threshold afterward so later tests are not affected.
      ⍝
      OldThresh←⎕FIO[202] '+'
      OldThresh ⎕FIO[202] '+'
8888888888888888888

⍝ regression (Bugs28 #76): ⎕FIO[22] (fprintf) to a handle open for
⍝ reading only (handle 0, stdin) used to raise SYSTEM ERROR from the
⍝ generic fwrite()-failed check in do_fprintf(). A failed fwrite() on a
⍝ non-writable stream is a normal, expectable I/O outcome, not an
⍝ internal error -- report it the same way the other ⎕FIO write
⍝ functions (e.g. ⎕FIO[7]) report a failed write: return 0 (nothing
⍝ written) and leave errno for ⎕FIO[1] to inspect, instead of throwing.
⍝
      ('%d' 5) ⎕FIO[22] 0
0

⍝     )VARS

⍝ ==================================

